# SUB source files
#
# GitHub highlights code with Linguist, which only knows the languages in its
# own languages.yml. SUB is not one of them, and there is no way to hand
# GitHub a grammar from inside a repository -- .github/linguist/ is read by
# nothing. So `linguist-language=SUB` silently did nothing and .sb files
# rendered as plain text.
#
# Pointing at a language Linguist does know is what actually colours them.
# CoffeeScript is the closest fit by some margin: it is the only grammar that
# gets SUB's `#` comments, its strings, its numbers and most of its keywords
# (let, if, else, for, in, while, return, break, continue, switch, case, do,
# true, false, null), and it never mistakes SUB code for a string or a regex
# the way the Go grammar does. docs/SYNTAX_HIGHLIGHTING.md has the scores for
# every candidate; the workflow of the same name checks that whatever is named
# here still resolves to a real Linguist language with a real grammar.
#
# linguist-detectable=false keeps the alias out of the repository's language
# bar. The highlighting is worth borrowing; the label is not -- without this
# the front page would report a compiler written in C as a CoffeeScript
# project. Getting SUB itself into that bar means getting SUB into Linguist,
# which is gated on the language being in use across many repositories.
*.sb  linguist-language=CoffeeScript linguist-detectable=false
*.sub linguist-language=CoffeeScript linguist-detectable=false

# Documentation
*.md linguist-documentation=true

# Examples
examples/** linguist-documentation=true

# Generated files
*.o linguist-generated=true
*.exe linguist-generated=true
*.out linguist-generated=true
*.s linguist-generated=true
*.asm linguist-generated=true

# Vendored code (if any)
stdlib/** linguist-vendored=false
